    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d389-a7">

                <pb facs="0564" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg468"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Subinclosure No. 6.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1897-11-03"><hi rendition="#small-caps">November</hi> 3,
                            1897</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">New York Condensed Milk
                        Company</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">New York.</hi></p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sirs</hi>: In answer to yours of October 4, we
                    now have to regret to inform you that your trade-mark can not be registered in
                    Japan, although we think an appeal should be made against such a foolish
                    decision. In a long interview the writer (our Mr. Maurice Russell) had with Mr.
                    Nukiyama, he said nothing could be done, it being proved that for many years a
                    brand of milk known as the Eagle was manufactured and sold in Japan, and that
                    applications had been made to register the (false) label, but all applications
                    like the one shown for years had been refused. You will see that we have lost no
                    time in either applying or definitely forwarding you the reply, and can only
                    again offer our services on your behalf.</p>
                <p>Yours,</p>
                <closer><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. Curnow</hi> &amp; Co.,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">Yokohama.</hi></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
